course: dictionary
lesson: 5121
title: Invade: to enter by force or uninvited
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To invade means to go into a place, often by force, to take control of it. «In September 1939 Germany invaded Poland, and Britain and France declared war.» Here invade means an army marched into a country to take it over.
explain: Invade can also mean to come into a place in large numbers when nobody wanted you there. «Ants invaded the kitchen» tells you there were far too many ants, and nobody invited them. Ask yourself: is the thing coming in welcome, or is it an unwelcome crowd?
explain: You can also invade someone's privacy by poking into their things or getting too close to their personal space. Invade is a strong word, so writers choose it when something is wrong or unwelcome.
fact: Invade is a verb: invade, invades, invaded, invading.
fact: The noun is invasion, and a person who invades is an invader.
fact: «Kublai Khan's fleets twice tried to invade Japan, in 1274 and 1281, and both attempts failed, partly because of storms.»
fact: In science, germs and invasive species, like grey squirrels in Britain or cane toads in Australia, invade habitats that are not their own.
fact: Some facts are widely reported, like who invaded whom. Others are arguments people still have, so keep the two apart when you read.
exercise: «In 1939 Germany invaded Poland.» What does invaded mean here? | Sang a song about | *Entered with an army to take control | Painted a picture of | Left in a hurry with a suitcase
exercise: «Ants invaded the kitchen.» What does invaded mean? | Sat quietly on the table | Cleaned every crumb away | Politely asked to come in | *Came in in large numbers, unwelcome
exercise: Find and copy the noun that means the act of invading. | invaded | *invasion | invade | invades
test: Which word means a person who invades? | invading | invasion | *invader | invades
test: Ruby says someone poked through her diary, so they invaded her privacy. What does that mean? | They helped her tidy her room | *They came into her private things without permission | They were polite and asked first | They gave her a present
exam: Which sentence uses invade to mean come in large numbers, unwelcome? | *Wasps invaded the picnic, and nobody had asked them | Grandad invaded the garden with his best roses | The teacher invaded the lesson with a smile | Ella invited the class to her party
